<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#persian-miniature">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persian miniature</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as miniature painting</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Persian miniature is a tradition of small, detailed painting, usually used to illustrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of poetry and history. It flourished under Timurid patronage, especially at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, where the court produced works that influenced later Mughal and Safavid art. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a> continued the tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-miniature.jpg" alt="Persian miniature" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yusef_Zuleykha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
